# [[ .Name ]]

A quan. It cannot run on its own: a quanar loads it, runs it in its own process and does
its work. Everything it needs from the outside comes through its qua.
[[ if not .Quas ]]
> **No qua yet.** Add a qua to `package.json`, list it in `external` in `build.mjs`, and
> use its `api` and `db` in `src`.
[[ end ]]
- `src/index.ts` asks the quanar to listen: `api.get('/[[ .Name ]]', …)`, `api.post('/[[ .Name ]]', …)`.
  Every path starts with the quan's name.
- `src/handlers` take a request and return a response; they only call services.
- `src/services` hold the quan's rules and use the qua's `db`, naming the table themselves.

```sh
npm run watch    # builds, serves the file on port [[ .Port ]] and announces each new build at /events
```

The quanar follows `/events`: it loads the quan when the stream opens, reloads it on every
new build and unmounts it when the stream closes. Running `node dist/[[ .Name ]].js` alone does
nothing: the qua has no core until a quanar plugs one in.
